        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "e5e22197-ecec-5d88-82b7-eea0c356b755",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Events: 1854 Broad Street cholera outbreak, Golden Square outbreak\n- Places: Broad Street/Broadwick Street, Soho, London, England\n- People: John Snow\n- Topics: cholera, contaminated water, anaesthesia, medical hygiene\n\nFacts:\n- John Snow was an English physician born on 15 March 1813 and died on 16 June 1858.\n- John Snow was a leader in the adoption of anaesthesia and medical hygiene.\n- The 1854 Broad Street cholera outbreak, also called the Golden Square outbreak, was a severe cholera outbreak that occurred in 1854 near Broad Street, now Broadwick Street, in Soho, London, England.\n- The 1854 Broad Street cholera outbreak killed 616 people.\n- The 1854 Broad Street cholera outbreak is best known for John Snow's study of its causes and for his hypothesis that contaminated water rather than air was the source of cholera.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "0b7575f2-0121-52ef-884a-4d9ef037a2ec",
        "text": "Page: 1854 Broad Street cholera outbreak. Sentence: The Broad Street cholera outbreak (or Golden Square outbreak) was a severe outbreak of cholera that occurred in 1854 near Broad Street (now Broadwick Street) in the Soho district of London, England.\nPage: 1854 Broad Street cholera outbreak. Sentence: This outbreak, which killed 616 people, is best known for the physician John Snow's study of its causes and his hypothesis that contaminated water, not air, was the source of cholera.\nPage: John Snow. Sentence: John Snow (15 March 1813 – 16 June 1858) was an English physician and a leader in the adoption of anaesthesia and medical hygiene.",
